Efficient capture of \(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}\) from \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathbf{C}_{n}\mathbf{H}_{4}\) by a novel fluorinated anion pillared MOF with flexible molecular sieving effect
Lingyao Wang\(^{1}\), Nuo Xu\(^{1}\), Yongqi Hu\(^{1}\), Wanqi Sun\(^{1}\), Rajamari Krishna\(^{2}\), Jiahao Li\(^{1}\), Yunjia Jiang\(^{1}\), Simon Duttwyler\(^{3}\), and Yuanbin Zhang\(^{1}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) Key Laboratory of the Ministry of Education for Advanced Catalysis Materials, College of Chemistry and Life Sciences, Zhejiang Normal University, Jinhua 321004, China  \(^{2}\) Van't Hoff Institute for Molecular Sciences, University of Amsterdam, Science Park 904, Amsterdam 1098 XH, the Netherlands  \(^{3}\) Department of Chemistry, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou 310027, China
\(\circledcirc\) Tsinghua University Press 2022  Received: 12 July 2022 / Revised: 11 August 2022 / Accepted: 31 August 2022
## ABSTRACT
The efficient separation of acetylene \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\right)\) from carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\right)\) and \(\mathsf{C}_{n}\mathsf{H}_{4}\) \((n=1\) and 2) to manufacture high purity \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) and recover other light hydrocarbons is technologically important, while posing significant challenges. Herein, we reported a new \(\mathsf{T i F}_{6}{}^{2-}\) anion (TIFSIX) pillared metal-organic framework (MOF) ZNU-5 (ZNU = Zhejiang Normal University) with ultramicropores for highly selective \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) capture with low adsorption heat through gate opening based molecular sieving effect. ZNU-5 takes up a large amount of \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) \((128.6\;\mathsf{c m}^{3}/\mathsf{g})\) at 1.0 bar and \(298\;\mathsf{K}\) but excludes \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\), \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}\), and \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\). Such high capacity has never been realized in MOFs with molecular sieving. The breakthrough experiments further confirmed the highly selective \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) separation performance from multi-component gas mixtures. 3.3, 2.8, and \(2.2\,\mathsf{\ m m o l/g}\) of \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) is captured at ZNU-5 from equimolar \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}\), \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}\), and \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}\) mixtures, respectively. Furthermore, 2.6, 2.0, and \(1.5\;\mathsf{m m o l/g}\) of \(>98\%\) purity \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) can be recycled from the desorption process. Combining high working capacity, low adsorption heat, as well as good recyclability, ZNU-5 is promising for \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\) purification.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), \(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) separation, acetylene recovery, molecular sieving, flexible MOFs
## 1 Introduction
Acetylene \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\right)\) is a major raw feedstock for the production of various essential chemicals and polymers in industry [1–3]. It is produced from the partial combustion of natural gas or stream cracking of hydrocarbons, in which carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)\) and other \(\mathrm{C}_{1}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{2}\) light hydrocarbons are worth-noting contaminants that need to be removed to produce \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in high purity [4, 5]. Currently, energy-intensive cryogenic distillation and solvent extraction are employed for the recovery of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from other gases. Due to the close boiling points, these approaches suffer from low energy inefficiency and are environmentally unfriendly. Therefore, physicoactive separation using porous solid adsorbents has attracted particular interest based on the lower cost and energy consumption [6–16]. However, the similarities among these gas molecules in terms of molecular size (kinetic diameter: \(3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for both \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(3.8\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), and \(4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\)) and physical properties make these separations a great challenge [17–21].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are famous for their powerful structural predictability and tunability on pore size/shape and functionality [22–30]. However, the flexibility of MOFs is still very challenging to predict and flexible MOFs have less been studied in selective gas separation. UTSA-300 (SIFSIX-dps-Zn; SIFSIX = \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\), dps = 4,4'-dipyridylsulfide) [31] is the first
reported flexible MOF that takes up \(69.0\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and negligible \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\). By replacing the zinc ion \((\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})\) to copper ion \((\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})\), the resulting SIFSIX-dps-Cu [32] exhibits increased \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake of \(102.4\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) as well as an ultrahigh selectivity of 1,787. However, the practical dynamic capacity of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixture is only \(2.48\;\;\mathrm{mmol/g}\), even lower than that \((2.9\;\mathrm{mmol/g})\) of our recently reported robust MOF ZNU-1 (ZNU = Zhejiang Normal University) [33] with static uptake of \(76.3\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) and ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity of 56.6. Therefore, high practical working capacity is still very difficult to realize by flexible MOFs in the context of challenging \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation.
Herein, we would like to report a new \(\mathrm{TEf}_{6}{}^{2-}\) anion (TIFSIX) pillared flexible metal-organic framework ZNU-5 for selective \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption. ZNU-5 is constructed by self-assembly of \(\mathrm{CuTiF}_{6}\) and 1,4-di(1H-imidazol-1-yl)benzene (DIB) in \(\mathrm{MeOH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution. It displays a large capacity of \(128.6\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) under 1.0 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) but only adsorbs 15.2, 11.9, and \(3.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). The near-zero coverage \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption heat is as low as \(27.8\;\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\), indicative of its low energy footprint for material regeneration. The calculated IAST selectivities at 1.0 bar are 11.6 for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), 255 for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and 850 for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). Such high selectivities have rarely been achieved by reported top-performing MOFs. The practical separation performance is fully demonstrated by the breakthrough
experiments of multi-component gas mixtures. 3.3, 2.8, and \(2.2\;\mathrm{mmol/g}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is captured at ZNU-5 from \(50/50\;\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), 33.3/33.3/33.3 \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), and 25/25/25/25 \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) mixtures, respectively. 2.6, 2.0, and \(1.5\;\mathrm{mmol/g}\) of \(>98\%\) purity \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) can be recycled from the desorption process. No separation performance reduction is observed over 5 cycles. Therefore, combining high working capacity, low adsorption heat, as well as good recyclability, ZNU-5 is promising for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) purification.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of ZNU-5
To a \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) long thin tube was added \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of aqueous solution with \((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{TiF}_{6}\) \(\left(1~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(1~\mathrm{mg}\right)\). \(3\,\mathrm{\mL}\) of \(\mathrm{MeOH/H_{2}O}\) mixture was slowly layered above the solution, followed by \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of MeOH solution of 1,4-di(1H-imidazol-1- yl)benzene \(\left(2\,\mathrm{\mg}\right)\). The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at room temperature. After several days, purple needle-shaped crystals were formed on the glass surface. The average yield was ca. \(75\%\).
## 2.2 Synthesis of ZNU-4
To a \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) long thin tube was added \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of aqueous solution with \((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{TiF}_{6}\) \(\left(1~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(1~\mathrm{mg}\right)\). \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{MeCN/H_{2}O}\) mixture was slowly layered above the solution, followed by \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of MeCN solution of 1,4-di(1H-imidazol-1- yl)benzene \(\left(2~\mathrm{mg}\right)\). The tube was sealed and left undisturbed at room temperature. After several days, blue flake shaped crystals were formed on the glass surface. The average yield was ca. \(75\%\).
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structural analysis and characterization
linkers generate a very twisted loop. Every unit cell contains two adjacent narrow one-dimensional (1D) channel with fluorine atoms decorated in the surface (Fig. 1(c)). The solvents play an important role in the self-assembly of the building units. In \(\mathrm{MeOH/H_{2}O}\) solution, needle-shaped purple crystals of ZNU-5 were cultivated with distinct porous structures (Fig. S2(b) in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 1(d), every \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) cation is octahedrally coordinated to four DIB ligands in half syn and half anti configuration and two TIFSIX anions in cis coordination mode, extending to a non-interpenetrated pcu topology framework (Fig. 1(e)). Different from ZNU-4, ZNU-5 features two-dimensional pore channels as shown in Fig. 1(f).
As shown in Fig. 2(a), the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the as-synthesized ZNU-5 are consistent with the simulated ones from crystal structure, indicating the pure phase of sample. ZNU-5 is relatively stable in humid air but sensitive to water. A new phase appears when the sample is immersed in water. Interestingly, the original phase can be recovered when the water-soaked samples were re-activated or re-soaked in methanol (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA) analysis was conducted to compare the thermal stability of ZNU-4 and ZNU-5 qualitatively (Fig. 2(b)). The weight reduction before \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) belongs to the loss of water and organic solvent in the pores. ZNU-5 shows a second weight loss until \(340~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), slightly higher than that of ZNU-4 \((290~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), suggesting the superior thermal stability of ZNU-5.
## 3.2 Single-component adsorption experiments and selectivity calculations
To evaluate the pore properties of ZNU-4 and ZNU-5, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption measurements were performed at \(195~\mathrm{K}\) (Fig. 3(a)). ZNU-4 displays the type-I isotherm and the maximum loading is \(105.7\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(100\;\;\mathrm{kPa}\). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area is \(358.6\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\). In contrast, two stages of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption are observed for ZNU-5. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptakes in the first and second steps are 200.9 \((15\;\mathrm{kPa})\) and \(299.8\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) \((100\;\mathrm{kPa})\), respectively. The BET surface area is \(751.5\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Fig. S14(b) in the ESM). The calculated pore width is \(5.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) (Fig. S14(a) in the ESM), perfectly consistent with the pore size \((5.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) measured from the single crystal structure (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
The distinct structure/pore architectures and porosity of ZNU-4 and ZNU-5 prompted us to evaluate their difference in gas
adsorption and separation performances. As shown in Figs. 3(b)–3(d), and Figs. S15 and S16 in the ESM, we performed unary gas adsorption tests for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) under the temperature ranging from 263 to \(313\,\mathrm{~K~}\) . The \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms of ZNU-5 showed gate-opening pressure increases from \(5\ \mathrm{kPa}\) (273 K) to around \(20\ \mathrm{kPa}\) (298 K) and \(30\ \mathrm{kPa}\) (313 K), indicating that the gate-opening pressure is temperature dependent. Such temperature dependent gate-opening has also been observed for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \(263\ \mathrm{K},\) which possesses a higher gateopening pressure \((65~\mathrm{\kPa})\) . Impressively, ZNU-5 exhibits a remarkably high \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake of \(128.6\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(100\,\mathrm{kPa}\) with a flexible feature (Fig. 3(c)), which is \(51.1\%\) higher than that of ZNU-4 \((85.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) . This value sets a new record of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity at \(100\;\;\mathrm{kPa}\) among MOF materials with \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecular sieving effect, which outperforms many benchmark MOFs including UTSA-300 \((69.0\ \mathrm{\cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [31], NTU-65 (75.4 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [35], ZJU-196 \((83.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [36], CPL- \(1\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) \((41.2\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [37], SIFSIX-dps-Cu \((102.4\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [32], and MOF-OH (60.0 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) [38]. Notably, ZNU-5 completely prevents the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) entrance at the pressure up to \(100\;\;\mathrm{kPa}\) at \(298\;\mathrm{~K~}\) . Therefore, the exceptional \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capture capacity at \(100\;\;\mathrm{kPa}\) combined with the much lower \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) uptakes enables ZNU-5 with great potential to achieve one-step \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) purification from quaternary \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C H_{4}}\) mixtures.
Figure 4(a) shows that the initial \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((27.8\;\;\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) in ZNU-5 is much lower than that of ZNU-4 \((50.3\,\mathrm{\kJ/mol})\) , implying a relatively low energy consumption in regeneration.
IAST calculations were performed to qualitatively evaluate the adsorption selectivity of ZNU-4 and ZNU-5 for equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) mixtures at \(298\ \mathrm{~K},\) respectively. Due to the existence of the gate-opening phenomenon, the fitting is challenging and temperature dependant dual-site Langmuir–Freundlich model is applied. The comparison of experimental and simulated adsorption isotherms
as well as the fitting parameters are presented in Fig. S17 and Table S3 in the ESM, which showed the fitting is in excellent accuracy. For equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) mixtures, as presented in Figs. 4(b)–4(d), ZNU-5 exhibits a higher selectivity up to 12, 255, and 850 at \(100\;\mathrm{kPa},\) which is superior to ZNU-4 with the selectivity of 9, 12, and 300.
## 3.3 Dynamic breakthrough experiments
Transient breakthrough simulations were conducted to evaluate the separation performance of ZNU-5 for equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (50/50) mixture. As shown in Fig. 6, ZNU-5 exhibits a stepped breakthrough curve for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) which is not seen in rigid adsorbents [54]. Nonetheless, the captured \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) amount is still very large while that of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is negligible, thus leading to a high separation factor. Besides, as \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) the target gas, needs desorption process to obtain, slight leakage in the breakthrough process will not have large influence on the dynamic capacity of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) .
To evaluate the practical separation performance as well as confirm the stepped breakthrough phenomenon, experimental breakthrough tests were conducted. The results showed that the experimental breakthrough curves are very similar with the simulations (Fig. S18 in the ESM). For equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixtures, efficient separations could be accomplished by ZNU-5
with \(3.3\;\mathrm{mol/g}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity, while the uptake of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) absorbed in ZNU-4 was only \(2.2\;\mathrm{mol/g}\) (Fig. 7(a)). Subsequently, we explored the effect of different desorption temperatures on regeneration. Figure 7(b) shows that the desorption time of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is gradually shortened with the increase of desorption temperature. Controlling the desorption temperature of \(25~^{\circ}\!C,\) \(2.6\;\mathrm{mol/g}\) of \(98\%\) purity \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) can be recovered from the column after blowing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) out at the first stage. The dynamic separation
factor of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is calculated to be 9.1 (Fig. S20 in the ESM), higher than those of ZNU-4 (5.4) [34] and many other top-performing materials such as CAU-10-H (3.4) [53], JCM-1 (4.4) [45], ZJU-74a (4.3) [55], and SNU-45 (2.9) [8]. The capture ability of ZNU-5 for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from the ternary and quaternary mixtures was further studied. As shown in Figs. 7(c) and 7(d), when the gas mixtures containing equal ratios of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) or \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}/C H_{4}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) passed through the ZNU-5 packed column, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) or \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) outflowed first, then \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) began to discharge at around \(104/170\,\mathrm{\min},\) and up to \(2.8/2.2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was adsorbed in this process. During desorption process, \(2.0/1.5\ \mathrm{mmol/g}\) of \(98\%\) purity \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) can be recovered from the column by stepped Ar purge process, which are both higher than those of ZNU-4 (6.0.4 and \(0.05\,\mathrm{mmol/g})\) [34].
Recycability is a very important parameter to assess the potential for practical application. Thus, 5 repetitive breakthrough experiments were carried out. Negligible capacity reduction is observed (Fig. 8), demonstrating that ZNU-5 is a promising adsorbent with good cyclic utilization performance.
revealed that ZNU-5 exhibits not only high \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacity but also simultaneously excellent \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (12), \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) (355), and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}\) (850) selectivity under ambient conditions, outperforming most of the flexible molecular sieving MOFs reported. Experimental breakthrough tests further confirmed the effective capture of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from binary \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) ternary \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}/C H_{4}},\) and quaternary \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}/C H_{4}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures with large productivity and good recyclability. Thus, combining large working capacity, low adsorption heat, as well as excellent recyclability, ZNU-5 is a promising adsorbent for practical \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) purification and separation. The mechanism of the selective gate opening behavior is under study.
## 4 Conclusions
In a nutshell, we reported a novel fluorinated anion pillared MOF with flexible molecular sieving effect that can efficiently capture \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) \((n=1\) and 2). The foregoing results
